    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go deeper into the specific failure modes when the thermal management fails? I'm particularly interested in what happens when the heat buildup becomes unpredictable or when the system seems to \"misbehave\" despite the theoretical cooling calculations. What are the primary indicators of unexpected thermal instability in these materials?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When thermal management fails, the brake system can exhibit a range of failure modes, including thermal runaway, thermal shock, and thermal fatigue. In the case of carbon-ceramic pads, thermal runaway can occur when the heat buildup becomes too great, causing the pad material to degrade rapidly. This can lead to a sudden and unpredictable increase in rotor temperature, which can result in a loss of braking performance or even a catastrophic failure of the brake system.\n\nThermal shock, on the other hand, occurs when the brake system is subjected to rapid temperature changes, causing the materials to expand and contract rapidly. This can lead to a loss of bond strength between the pad and the rotor, resulting in a decrease in braking performance or even a failure of the brake system.\n\nThermal fatigue, which is a more gradual process, occurs when the brake system is subjected to repeated thermal cycles, causing the materials to degrade over time. This can lead to a decrease in braking performance and a loss of confidence in the brake system.\n\nIn the case of semi-metallic pads, thermal instability can manifest in different ways. One common indicator is the development of thermal hotspots, where the pad material becomes overheated and degrades rapidly. This can lead to a loss of braking performance",
      "source": "target",
